Answer: Separation of concerns means placing different tasks in different methods. For example:

```java
add();
subtract();
multiply();
divide();
```

Each method handles one particular operation. This avoids placing all the logic inside main() and keeps the program organised.

Answer: Code reusability means using an existing method again instead of rewriting the same logic. For example, an addition method can be reused while calculating an average:

```java
public static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println(add(10, 20) / 2);
}
```

The add() method returns 30, and 30 / 2 produces 15. The addition logic does not need to be written again.

Answer: When the calling method and the called static method are in the same class, the class name can be omitted. Both forms are valid:

```java
add(10, 20);
Sample.add(10, 20);
```

When the method is in another class, the class name is required:

```java
SampleOne.add(10, 20);
```

